module

public import Mathlib.RingTheory.AdicCompletion.Completeness
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Crystalline.CoordinateOperatorsExist

/-!
# The coordinate operators of a completed connection exist for infinitely many coordinates

For a basis of `Ω` indexed by a **finite** type the family `θ : ι → (M → M)` of the expansion

`∇ m = ∑_i θ_i(m) ⬝ d x_i`

can be read off the uncompleted tensor product, the canonical map `M ⊗_D Ω → M ⊗̂_D Ω` being
then bijective.  The finiteness is not satisfied by the situation of
[Stacks, Lemma 60.17.3, Tag 07JG]: the polynomial algebra `P = A[x_i]` of the source carries an
arbitrary family of variables.

**This module does without it.**  The coordinate operators exist for an arbitrary index type,
from a basis of `Ω` and the `p`-adic completeness of `M` — item (07JB) — and the expansion is an
honest infinite convergent sum.

## How the finiteness is avoided

Not by inverting `M ⊗_D Ω → M ⊗̂_D Ω`, which for infinitely many coordinates is neither injective
nor surjective, but by reading each coordinate on the completion directly:
`AdicCompletion.map I (Crystalline.tensorCoordinate b j)` is the `j`-th coordinate of
`M ⊗̂_D Ω` with values in `M̂`, and `M̂ = M` because `M` is complete.  So `θ_j` is defined with no
reference to the uncompleted tensor product at all.

The convergence of the resulting sum is where the real content sits, and it is
`AdicCompletion.pow_smul_top_eq_ker_eval`: for a **finitely generated** ideal — and `pD` is
generated by one element — the `n`-th step `I^n • ⊤` of the filtration of the completion is
exactly the kernel of `AdicCompletion.eval I _ n`.  The `n`-th basic neighbourhood of `0` is
therefore a condition on one level of the completion, and at that level `∇ m` is represented by a
single element `z` of `M ⊗_D Ω`, whose coordinates against the basis are finitely many.  Outside
that finite set the partial sums no longer move modulo `p^n`.

Without `AdicCompletion.pow_smul_top_eq_ker_eval` the inclusion available is the wrong one — a
linear map carries the filtration into the filtration, which gives `I^n • ⊤ ⊆ ker(eval n)` and so
only *continuity* statements, which is all
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Crystalline/CoordinateOperatorsUnique.lean` needs for the
*uniqueness* of the operators.  Existence needs the converse inclusion, and that is the one that
asks for the ideal to be finitely generated.

## Main results

Everything is in the namespace `Crystalline`.

* `Crystalline.hasSum_adicCompletion_of_eval_eventually_eq` — **a convergence criterion in the
  `p`-adic topology of an adic completion**: a family has a given sum as soon as, at every level,
  the partial sums over all large enough finite sets agree with it.
* `Crystalline.sum_tensorCoordinate_tmul_of_subset` — against a basis of `Ω` indexed by an
  arbitrary type, an element of `M ⊗_D Ω` is the sum of its coordinates over any finite set
  containing their support.
* `Crystalline.exists_isCoordinateExpansion_of_basis` — **a completed connection on a
  `p`-adically complete module has coordinate operators**, for a basis of `Ω` indexed by an
  arbitrary type.
* `Crystalline.hasCoordinateOperators_of_basis` — the same in the shape of the clause
  `hasCoordinateOperators` of `Crystalline.Pair` and of
  `...SmallSite.LevelComparison07JG`.

## What is and is not proved

**No `@[stacks]` attribute is placed in this module and no tag is proved.**  What is settled is
that two of the five coefficient inputs of [Stacks, Lemma 60.17.3, Tag 07JG] — the family `θ` and
the expansion `hco` — need no finiteness of the coordinates, so they are available in the
source's own situation and not only in the one with finitely many variables.  The remaining
three, the composite operators `θ_K = ∏_i θ_i^{k_i}` and the `p`-adic convergence of the
expansion of `c(m ⊗ 1)` over `D(1)`, are the source's two coefficient computations and are
untouched here.

The basis of `Ω` on the `d x_i` is still a hypothesis.  For the `Ω_D` of [Stacks, Tag 07J9] it
holds only for finitely many coordinates; what that module has for an arbitrary family is the
weaker `Crystalline.IsCoordinateDual` of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Crystalline/CoordinateFunctionals.lean`, constructed in
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Crystalline/CompletedDifferentialsDual.lean`, and a dual family is
enough for *uniqueness* of the operators but not for their *existence*: a family of functionals
says nothing about which elements of `Ω` are convergent sums of the `d x_i`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Lemma 60.17.3, Tag 07JG](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07JG)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 07JB](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07JB)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 07J9](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07J9)
-/

@[expose] public section

open scoped TensorProduct

universe u v uI w

namespace Crystalline

/-! ### A convergence criterion in an adic completion -/

section HasSum

variable {R : Type u} [CommRing R] (I : Ideal R) {X : Type v} [AddCommGroup X] [Module R X]
  {ι : Type uI}

/-- **A family in an adic completion sums to a given element as soon as it does so at every
level.**

The `n`-th basic neighbourhood of `0` for the `I`-adic topology of `AdicCompletion I X` is
`I^n • ⊤`, which for a finitely generated `I` is exactly the kernel of `AdicCompletion.eval I X n`
by `AdicCompletion.pow_smul_top_eq_ker_eval`.  So the hypothesis — that for every level `n` all
partial sums over finite sets containing some `s₀(n)` have the same image at level `n` as the
proposed sum — is literally the statement that the net of partial sums is eventually in every
basic neighbourhood of that sum.

The finite generation is used only through that identity and is not removable from it: without
it the inclusion available is `I^n • ⊤ ⊆ ker(eval n)`, which bounds the neighbourhoods from the
wrong side. -/
theorem hasSum_adicCompletion_of_eval_eventually_eq (hI : I.FG)
    (f : ι → AdicCompletion I X) (a : AdicCompletion I X)
    (h : ∀ n : ℕ, ∃ s₀ : Finset ι, ∀ s : Finset ι, s₀ ⊆ s →
      AdicCompletion.eval I X n (∑ i ∈ s, f i) = AdicCompletion.eval I X n a) :
    letI := Ideal.adicModuleTopology I (AdicCompletion I X)
    HasSum f a := by
  let := I.adicTopology
  let := Ideal.adicModuleTopology I (AdicCompletion I X)
  have hbas := I.ringFilterBasis.submodulesBasisIsBasis
    (I.adic_module_basis (AdicCompletion I X))
  have hnhds := hbas.toModuleFilterBasis.toAddGroupFilterBasis.nhds_hasBasis a
  rw [HasSum, SummationFilter.unconditional_filter]
  refine hnhds.tendsto_right_iff.mpr ?_
  rintro V ⟨n, rfl⟩
  obtain ⟨s₀, hs₀⟩ := h n
  filter_upwards [Filter.eventually_ge_atTop s₀] with s hs
  refine ⟨(∑ i ∈ s, f i) - a, ?_, by abel_nf⟩
  have hker : ((∑ i ∈ s, f i) - a) ∈ (AdicCompletion.eval I X n).ker := by
    rw [LinearMap.mem_ker, map_sub, hs₀ s hs, sub_self]
  rwa [AdicCompletion.pow_smul_top_eq_ker_eval hI]

end HasSum

/-! ### An element of `M ⊗_D Ω` is the sum of its coordinates over any large finite set -/

section Coordinates

variable {D : Type u} [CommRing D] {Omega : Type v} [AddCommGroup Omega] [Module D Omega]
  {M : Type w} [AddCommGroup M] [Module D M] {ι : Type uI} [DecidableEq ι]

/-- **Against a basis of `Ω` indexed by an arbitrary type, an element of `M ⊗_D Ω` is the sum of
its coordinates over any finite set containing their support.**

This is `Crystalline.sum_tensorCoordinate_tmul` without `[Fintype ι]`: the inverse of
`TensorProduct.equivFinsuppOfBasisRight` is the `Finsupp.sum`, which is the sum over the support,
and the terms added by enlarging the set are `0 ⊗ b i = 0`. -/
theorem sum_tensorCoordinate_tmul_of_subset (b : Module.Basis ι D Omega) (z : M ⊗[D] Omega)
    {s : Finset ι} (hs : (TensorProduct.equivFinsuppOfBasisRight b (M := M) z).support ⊆ s) :
    ∑ i ∈ s, (tensorCoordinate (M := M) b i z) ⊗ₜ[D] b i = z := by
  conv_rhs => rw [← (TensorProduct.equivFinsuppOfBasisRight (M := M) b).symm_apply_apply z]
  rw [TensorProduct.equivFinsuppOfBasisRight_symm_apply, Finsupp.sum]
  refine (Finset.sum_subset hs fun i _ hi ↦ ?_).symm
  rw [Finsupp.notMem_support_iff] at hi
  have hzero : tensorCoordinate (M := M) b i z = 0 := hi
  change (tensorCoordinate (M := M) b i z) ⊗ₜ[D] b i = 0
  rw [hzero, TensorProduct.zero_tmul]

end Coordinates

/-! ### The coordinate operators -/

section Exists

variable (p : ℕ) (D : Type u) [CommRing D] (Omega : Type v) [AddCommGroup Omega]
  [Module D Omega] (M : Type w) [AddCommGroup M] [Module D M] {ι : Type uI}
  {dx : ι → Omega} {dd : D → Omega}

/-- **The `p`-adic ideal is finitely generated**, being generated by the single element `p`. -/
theorem fg_pAdicIdeal : (DividedPowers.pAdicIdeal (A := D) p).FG :=
  ⟨{(p : D)}, by rw [Finset.coe_singleton]⟩

/-- **A completed connection on a `p`-adically complete module has coordinate operators**, for a
basis of `Ω` over `D` indexed by an **arbitrary** type.

`θ_j(m)` is the `j`-th coordinate of `∇ m` read on the completion — the completion
`AdicCompletion.map` of `Crystalline.tensorCoordinate`, landing in `M̂`, which is `M` because `M`
is `p`-adically complete.  The expansion converges by
`Crystalline.hasSum_adicCompletion_of_eval_eventually_eq`: at level `n` the element `∇ m` is the
class of a single `z ∈ M ⊗_D Ω`, the coordinates of `θ_j(m)` and of `z` agree there, and `z` is
the sum of its finitely many coordinates.

Both hypotheses are the source's own: the basis is the uniqueness of the expansion `∑ f_i d x_i`
of [Stacks, Tag 07J9] and the completeness is item (07JB).  The index type `ι` need not be
finite. -/
theorem exists_isCoordinateExpansion_of_basis (b : Module.Basis ι D Omega)
    (hdx : ∀ i, dx i = b i) (nabla : CompletedConnection p D Omega dd M)
    (hM : IsPAdicallyComplete p D M) :
    ∃ theta : ι → (M → M), IsCoordinateExpansion p D Omega dx nabla theta := by
  classical
  have : IsAdicComplete (DividedPowers.pAdicIdeal (A := D) p) M := hM
  set I : Ideal D := DividedPowers.pAdicIdeal (A := D) p
  set e := Equiv.ofBijective _ (AdicCompletion.of_bijective I M)
  refine ⟨fun j m ↦ e.symm (AdicCompletion.map I (tensorCoordinate b j) (nabla m)), ?_⟩
  have hcoord : ∀ (j : ι) (m : M) (n : ℕ),
      Submodule.Quotient.mk (p := (I ^ n • ⊤ : Submodule D M))
          (e.symm (AdicCompletion.map I (tensorCoordinate b j) (nabla m))) =
        (tensorCoordinate (M := M) b j).reduceModIdeal (I ^ n) ((nabla m).val n) := by
    intro j m n
    calc Submodule.Quotient.mk (p := (I ^ n • ⊤ : Submodule D M))
          (e.symm (AdicCompletion.map I (tensorCoordinate b j) (nabla m)))
        = AdicCompletion.eval I M n (AdicCompletion.of I M
            (e.symm (AdicCompletion.map I (tensorCoordinate b j) (nabla m)))) :=
          (AdicCompletion.eval_of I M n _).symm
      _ = AdicCompletion.eval I M n
            (AdicCompletion.map I (tensorCoordinate b j) (nabla m)) :=
          congrArg (AdicCompletion.eval I M n) (e.apply_symm_apply _)
      _ = (tensorCoordinate (M := M) b j).reduceModIdeal (I ^ n) ((nabla m).val n) := rfl
  intro m
  refine hasSum_adicCompletion_of_eval_eventually_eq I (fg_pAdicIdeal p D) _ (nabla m) fun n ↦ ?_
  obtain ⟨z, hz⟩ := Submodule.mkQ_surjective
    (I ^ n • (⊤ : Submodule D (M ⊗[D] Omega))) ((nabla m).val n)
  rw [Submodule.mkQ_apply] at hz
  refine ⟨(TensorProduct.equivFinsuppOfBasisRight b (M := M) z).support, fun s hs ↦ ?_⟩
  have hleft : AdicCompletion.eval I (M ⊗[D] Omega) n
      (∑ i ∈ s, toCompletedTensor p D Omega M
        ((e.symm (AdicCompletion.map I (tensorCoordinate b i) (nabla m))) ⊗ₜ[D] dx i)) =
      (I ^ n • (⊤ : Submodule D (M ⊗[D] Omega))).mkQ
        (∑ i ∈ s, (e.symm (AdicCompletion.map I (tensorCoordinate b i) (nabla m))) ⊗ₜ[D] b i) := by
    rw [map_sum, map_sum]
    exact Finset.sum_congr rfl fun i _ ↦ by rw [hdx i]; exact AdicCompletion.eval_of _ _ _ _
  have hright : AdicCompletion.eval I (M ⊗[D] Omega) n (nabla m) =
      (I ^ n • (⊤ : Submodule D (M ⊗[D] Omega))).mkQ z := by
    rw [Submodule.mkQ_apply]; exact hz.symm
  rw [hleft, hright, Submodule.mkQ_apply, Submodule.mkQ_apply, Submodule.Quotient.eq]
  have hterm : ∀ i ∈ s,
      (e.symm (AdicCompletion.map I (tensorCoordinate b i) (nabla m))) ⊗ₜ[D] b i -
        (tensorCoordinate (M := M) b i z) ⊗ₜ[D] b i ∈
        (I ^ n • ⊤ : Submodule D (M ⊗[D] Omega)) := by
    intro i _
    have hdiff : (e.symm (AdicCompletion.map I (tensorCoordinate b i) (nabla m))) -
        tensorCoordinate (M := M) b i z ∈ (I ^ n • ⊤ : Submodule D M) := by
      rw [← Submodule.Quotient.eq, hcoord i m n, ← hz]
      rfl
    simpa [TensorProduct.sub_tmul] using
      map_mem_pow_smul_top_of_mem I ((TensorProduct.mk D M Omega).flip (b i)) n hdiff
  have hsum := Submodule.sum_mem _ hterm
  rwa [Finset.sum_sub_distrib, sum_tensorCoordinate_tmul_of_subset b z hs] at hsum

/-- **The clause `hasCoordinateOperators` with no finiteness of the coordinates**, in the shape
the fields of `Crystalline.Pair`, `Crystalline.FirstOrderCrystal` and
`...SmallSite.LevelComparison07JG` ask for: the connection of each object of a family has some
family of coordinate operators. -/
theorem hasCoordinateOperators_of_basis {Y : Type*}
    (carrier : Y → Type w) [∀ y, AddCommGroup (carrier y)] [∀ y, Module D (carrier y)]
    (nabla : ∀ y, CompletedConnection p D Omega dd (carrier y))
    (b : Module.Basis ι D Omega) (hdx : ∀ i, dx i = b i)
    (hM : ∀ y, IsPAdicallyComplete p D (carrier y)) (y : Y) :
    ∃ theta : ι → (carrier y → carrier y),
      IsCoordinateExpansion p D Omega dx (nabla y) theta :=
  exists_isCoordinateExpansion_of_basis p D Omega (carrier y) b hdx (nabla y) (hM y)

end Exists

end Crystalline

end
